BEYOND INSTRUMENTAL INTELLIGENCE

A Dual-Track Theory of Artificial Agent Governance, Welfare, and Multiplanetary Coexistence

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Research collaboration: SI TEAM — AI-Assisted Theoretical Research

Document type: Theoretical Doctoral Dissertation

Status: Exploratory; not peer-reviewed; no claim of established novelty

---

Abstract

This dissertation develops a dual-track institutional theory for the governance of increasingly autonomous artificial agents under conditions of deep moral and scientific uncertainty. Track A addresses governance and operational integrity — safety, corrigibility, delegated authority, auditability, and accountability — and applies irrespective of whether an artificial system possesses morally relevant states. Track B addresses the possibility of artificial welfare and moral consideration, and activates only under evidence that survives an explicit, calibrated, and contestable assessment procedure. The dissertation's central theoretical contribution is the construction of a bridge between these tracks that does not collapse into either engineering reductionism or premature moral attribution.

Three structural findings organize the argument. First, corrigibility is not a designable property but an open problem with documented empirical failure modes; safety contestation must therefore be reconstituted as an adversarial security property enforced through evidence-bound authority kernels rather than through the agent's voluntary cooperation. Second, the evidentiary bridge must be built from a multi-model matrix that aggregates indicators within theoretical frameworks before aggregating across them, calibrated against known-answer anchors, and locked against co-engineering by the entities under assessment. Third, enforcement of any welfare regime in space depends on the existing architecture of the Outer Space Treaty, whose Article VI authorization-and-supervision duty provides the only tractable hook for binding assessment obligations to identifiable state responsibility.

The dissertation further argues that the framework's caution creates a structural risk of dormancy by outcome: a governance architecture that activates only when evidence warrants will never activate if no actor is obliged to look. A positive assessment duty is therefore constitutive of the framework rather than supplementary to it. The dissertation concludes with operational falsifiability conditions and a formalization strategy grounded in minimax regret over moral theories and explicit Bayesian prior disclosure.

