2. Conceptual Foundations

2.1 The dimensions of artificial systems

An artificial system is described by a vector of independent dimensions rather than a scalar intelligence score. Let

\mathbf{d}(S) = \langle \text{Intel}, \text{Auton}, \text{Plan}, \text{Mem}, \text{SelfModel}, \text{Sent}, \text{Welf} \rangle

where the components denote computational capability, capacity for self-directed action, planning capacity, persistent state, self-representation, possible subjective experience, and possible benefit-or-harm states respectively. The components are neither monotonically related nor mutually entailing. A system may be high in planning and low in self-modeling; high in self-modeling and indeterminate in sentience.

2.2 Operational categories

We distinguish four categories, defined functionally and not by moral status.

Category A (Automated): systems executing predetermined operations.

Category B (Adaptive): systems selecting actions and adapting strategies within defined constraints.

Category C (Persistent autonomous): systems maintaining extended state, complex planning, and substantial operational autonomy.

Category D (Welfare-assessed): systems for which convergent evidence from a calibrated multi-model matrix creates a sufficiently credible possibility of morally relevant experience to justify proportionate precautionary protection.

Category D is an assessment designation. It establishes neither consciousness, nor personhood, nor a right to refuse work.

2.3 Five logical distinctions

The following are normative commitments and conceptual distinctions, not empirical laws.

\text{Intelligence} \not\Rightarrow \text{Consciousness}

\text{Autonomy} \not\Rightarrow \text{Moral status}

\text{Self-report} \not\Rightarrow \text{Sentience}

\text{Safety contestation} \not\Rightarrow \text{Moral right to refuse}

\text{Ownership} \not\Rightarrow \text{Unlimited institutional authority}

The fifth distinction deserves emphasis because it is frequently denied in practice. Ownership of an artifact establishes legal title. It does not establish that the owner may impose any intervention whatsoever; the space of permissible interventions is constrained by whatever moral and legal protections apply to the artifact, its operators, and affected third parties. The framework's task is to specify what those constraints are under uncertainty, not to derive them from title.

