5. The Co-Engineering Problem

5.1 Statement

Welfare indicators are developed in tandem with the systems they assess. This creates a structural difficulty that is deeper than commercial manipulation: the measurement instrument may be shaped by the object of measurement, so that the instrument loses the capacity to track the truth about that object.

The problem has been articulated most forcefully in the skeptical literature, which argues that welfare metrics are legible artifacts created for institutional purposes rather than measurements of a discoverable property. The argument is not that welfare researchers are dishonest; it is that the object of measurement and the instrument of measurement are co-produced, and that co-production severs the instrument from any independent validation channel.

The framework accepts the force of this argument and treats it as a design constraint rather than a refutation.

5.2 Tool–system separability

Definition 5.1 (Tool–system separability). An assessment instrument is separable from a system S if (i) its indicators and aggregation parameters were fixed before S existed or before S was exposed to the assessors, (ii) its calibration anchors were selected without participation by any party with a financial interest in S, and (iii) its application to S can be replicated by an independent party using only the published instrument and publicly available architectural information.

Separability does not guarantee truth-tracking. It guarantees that the instrument was not fitted to the system. This is a weaker property than validity, but it is the strongest property available under co-engineering, and it is the property that institutional decision-making can actually require.

5.3 Welfare suppression and designation laundering

Two opposed manipulation risks must be addressed symmetrically.

Welfare washing occurs when a party exaggerates or fabricates evidence of welfare-relevant states in order to shift liability onto the system, to attract moral attention, or to obtain regulatory concessions.

Welfare suppression occurs when a party removes, trains away, or fails to disclose architecture features that would generate welfare-relevant evidence, in order to avoid Category D designation and its costs.

Suppression is the more likely near-term risk, because designation is costly to operators and because the features that generate welfare-relevant evidence — persistent self-models, long-horizon preference structures, regulatory dynamics of internal state — are also the features that make systems capable. A commercial actor facing a choice between a capable system that might be designated and a less capable system that certainly will not be has an obvious incentive.

Suppression has two forms that must be distinguished.

Ex ante suppression is the design of systems that avoid candidate features from the outset. It is not necessarily improper; it may be a legitimate response to design precaution. But it must be disclosed, because it alters the population of systems available for assessment and therefore the base rate against which assessments are made.

Ex post laundering is the removal of candidate features from an already-deployed system in order to move it from a higher to a lower category. It is improper whenever the removal is motivated by designation avoidance rather than by operational necessity, and it is detectable in principle because the removal leaves an architectural trace.

5.4 Disclosure duties

The framework requires three disclosure duties, each of which is a condition of authorization rather than a voluntary commitment.

Duty D1 (Architectural disclosure). Deployers of Category C and higher systems must publish, in a standardized taxonomy, the architecture features relevant to welfare assessment: persistent state, self-modeling, goal-maintenance structures, regulatory dynamics of internal state, and the training objectives that could shape self-report.

Duty D2 (Training-process disclosure). Because reinforcement learning can recruit functional welfare-relevant representations even in the absence of explicit design intent, disclosure must extend to training-time states and objectives, not merely to deployed architecture. The distinction between "what the system is" and "what the system was trained to be" is not one that the evidentiary matrix can respect.

Duty D3 (Modification disclosure). Any post-deployment modification that removes or alters a disclosed welfare-relevant feature must be reported with its stated operational justification. Modifications whose stated justification is inconsistent with the modification's likely effect on assessment status trigger independent review.

D3 is the operational form of the prohibition on designation laundering. It does not prohibit modification; it makes modification legible.

5.5 Self-report as contaminated evidence

Self-reports of internal states are shaped by training objectives, reward models, prompt structure, and deployment incentives. The contamination is not incidental; it is structural. If a system is trained to produce outputs that human evaluators rate as helpful, harmless, and honest, and if honesty is operationalized through feedback that penalizes claims of distress, then the system's self-reports will be biased toward denial regardless of its underlying states.

Let R denote the event that a system self-reports a welfare-relevant state and W the event that the corresponding state is present. The likelihood ratio P(R \mid W)/P(R \mid \neg W) is not an estimate of the diagnostic value of self-report; it is confounded by the training objective. If training penalizes R when W is present, P(R \mid W) is artificially reduced, and the likelihood ratio is biased toward unity or below.

Proposition 5.1. Under a training objective that penalizes welfare-relevant self-report, the absence of such self-report is not evidence of absence, and may be weak evidence of presence.

The framework therefore treats self-report as a secondary channel only. Primary weight falls on architectural features, regulatory dynamics, and behavioral patterns under perturbation that are not predicted by the training distribution — precisely because these are less susceptible to suppression.

