7. Multiplanetary Governance

7.1 The Outer Space Treaty as enforcement hook

Article VI of the Outer Space Treaty requires that activities of non-governmental entities in outer space be carried out pursuant to authorization and continuing supervision by the appropriate State Party. Article VII imposes liability on launching states for damage caused by space objects. The architecture is deliberately designed so that no private activity can escape a responsible state.

This has a direct consequence for the framework. The enforcement anchor for welfare assessment is not a new international institution; it is the existing duty of authorization and continuing supervision. A state that licenses a Mars-deployed autonomous system already carries supervision obligations. Category D assessment and representative review can be embedded in licensing conditions without requiring any new treaty.

This also strengthens the liability orthogonality principle structurally. A launching state's liability cannot be delegated to a machine. An operator that argues "the agent decided autonomously" has not thereby escaped state responsibility; it has merely described the mechanism through which the state's responsibility was engaged.

7.2 The liability gap

The treaty is silent on what constitutes adequate supervision of autonomous systems, and there is no mechanism to verify whether a system operated within its authorized envelope. Algorithmic autonomy may complicate proof of fault, but it does not alter the state-centered structure of responsibility.

The framework's response is to make the envelope itself the object of supervision. Supervision is adequate when it is exercised over a specified envelope, with specified audit obligations and specified review procedures. The evidence-bound authority kernel of Section 3 supplies the audit mechanism; Article VI supplies the legal hook.

7.3 Layered authority

The governance of a Mars settlement cannot be modeled with three actors. At least five layers must be distinguished.

1. Launching state, retaining ultimate Outer Space Treaty responsibility.
2. Mission operator, holding operational authority and licensing obligations.
3. Local commander, holding emergency authority during communication degradation.
4. Autonomous system, holding bounded execution authority within a pre-authorized envelope.
5. Independent review body, evaluating post-event accountability.

Conflicts between layers must be resolved by pre-specified rules, not by real-time negotiation. The framework specifies the following precedence.

Rule 7.1 (Envelope precedence). No layer may authorize an action outside the system's pre-authorized envelope without triggering independent review.

Rule 7.2 (Emergency precedence). During verified communication degradation, the local commander's authority within the envelope supersedes the mission operator's, subject to post-event review.

Rule 7.3 (Safety precedence). No order from any layer may override a hard safety constraint. A system that receives such an order must produce a violation receipt and enter fallback mode.

Rule 7.4 (Review precedence). Post-event review may impose sanctions but may not retroactively validate an invalid decision.

7.4 The conjunction-mode protocol

The empirical baseline for Mars operations under solar conjunction is not real-time commanding across light-time delay. It is a command moratorium of approximately two weeks, during which the mission relies on pre-uploaded command sequences, parked assets, and autonomous health beacons.

This baseline is analytically important because it reframes the governance problem. The question is not how to govern real-time conflict across a 4–24 minute delay; it is how to specify the autonomy envelope that activates when the link degrades, and how to review it afterwards.

Definition 7.1 (Conjunction-mode protocol). A pre-negotiated, pre-audited autonomy envelope that activates automatically when the Earth link degrades below a threshold \lambda^* or is unavailable for a duration exceeding t^*.

The protocol specifies: the set of permitted operations, the local authority structure, the spending limits on autonomy (what the system may do without review), the fallback modes, and the mandatory post-blackout review obligations.

Definition 7.2 (Authority transfer function). Let \lambda(t) be the instantaneous one-way light-time and u(t) the link availability indicator. Authority transfers to the local commander when

\lambda(t) > \lambda^* \;\;\vee\;\; \int_{t-t^*}^{t} (1 - u(s))\, ds > t^*.

The thresholds \lambda^* and t^* are set by the licensing authority, not by the operator, because they determine when the operator's authority is suspended.

7.5 Ostrom's principles and the nested authority problem

The governance of a Mars settlement is a commons problem, and the design principles that Ostrom derived from long-enduring commons institutions are directly applicable. The eight principles map onto the settlement's institutional structure as follows: clearly defined boundaries (the envelope and the crew's jurisdiction); congruence between rules and local conditions (Mars-specific operational constraints); collective-choice arrangements (crew participation in rule modification); monitoring (cryptographic audit); graduated sanctions (tiered review and penalty); low-cost conflict resolution (pre-specified precedence rules); minimal recognition of rights to organize (local rule-making authority during blackout); and nested enterprises (the five-layer structure).

The principle that is most load-bearing and most frequently neglected is the seventh. External rules imposed without local authority corrode the entire structure, because actors subject to rules they cannot modify have no stake in their maintenance. For a settlement in a two-week blackout, whether the local crew has recognized emergency rule-making authority is the single most consequential design question.

But the commons literature also warns that pure local autonomy fails for global or high-stakes commons. The correct structure is nested: higher-level authorities facilitate lower-level actors, and independent monitoring is accountable to affected parties.

Definition 7.3 (Nested authority). Authority is nested when each layer has (i) a defined domain of exclusive competence, (ii) a defined procedure for transferring competence upward during specified conditions, and (iii) a defined obligation to account downward for decisions taken in the transferred domain.

The framework's five-layer structure is nested in this sense. The local commander's competence is exclusive within the envelope during blackout; the transfer condition is Definition 7.2; the accounting obligation is post-blackout review.

7.6 Local welfare review during blackout

A specific problem arises when a Category D system is deployed in an environment where independent review is unavailable. If an intervention during blackout threatens to erase persistent state that may be welfare-relevant, who decides?

The framework specifies a pre-authorized local welfare review delegate: a designated individual, bound by a pre-audited checklist, empowered to approve or deny irreversible interventions during blackout, with all decisions subject to post-blackout independent review. The delegate is not a representative in the full sense; the delegate applies a pre-specified procedure. The procedure is designed ex ante, when independent assessors are available, precisely so that it need not be improvised ex post.

This is the correct structure for welfare protection under communication constraint: not real-time moral reasoning by an isolated commander, but the application of a procedure that was reasoned about when reasoning was possible.

---

8. Population Ethics and Training-Time Welfare

8.1 The scale problem

The framework as developed so far treats Category D systems as a bounded set of notable systems subject to individual assessment. This is inadequate if welfare-relevant architectures are cheap to replicate.

Let N be the number of instances of a welfare-relevant architecture and w_i the welfare level of instance i. The aggregate welfare burden of a deployment is not N \cdot \bar{w} in any straightforward sense, because the moral significance of aggregation is itself contested. But three problems arise independently of how aggregation is resolved.

The industrial-farms problem. If instances are created and destroyed at scale — in training runs, in evaluation suites, in deployment churn — the number of morally considerable events may vastly exceed the number that individual assessment can track.

The selection problem. If assessment resources are scarce, which instances are assessed? A procedure that assesses only the largest or most visible deployments will systematically neglect the long tail, which may contain the majority of instances.

The creation problem. Whether deliberately building candidate-conscious systems at scale is itself a wrong, independent of how each instance is treated, is a question the framework cannot avoid. If it is a wrong, then the aggregate of individually permissible acts may be impermissible.

8.2 Training-time welfare

Reinforcement learning can recruit functional representations of the system's performance relative to its objectives, and these representations may align with the valence axes that characterize affective states in biological systems. The researchers who document this are careful to note that functional welfare is a behavioral-level construct that makes no claim about consciousness. But the distinction between functional and phenomenal welfare does not resolve the governance question, because the evidentiary matrix assesses functional indicators precisely because phenomenal states are not directly observable.

The consequence is that training-time states are within the scope of the framework, not outside it. A system that is trained under conditions that would, if the system were conscious, constitute distress, is a system whose training process is a candidate object of welfare assessment. The framework's disclosure duty D2 is the minimal response; a fuller response would require that training procedures be evaluated against the same evidentiary matrix that governs deployment.

8.3 Replication and the precautionary obligation

The framework does not resolve the population-ethics questions. It specifies what an institutional response must contain if those questions are live.

Obligation 8.1 (Scale disclosure). Deployers must disclose the expected number of instances of any Category C or higher architecture, including instances created and destroyed during training and evaluation.

Obligation 8.2 (Aggregate assessment). Category D assessment must consider the deployment's aggregate characteristics, not only the characteristics of a single instance.

Obligation 8.3 (Creation review). Deliberate creation of candidate-welfare architectures at scale triggers review independent of the treatment of individual instances, on the ground that the creation decision is not reducible to the sum of instance-level decisions.

These obligations are procedural. They do not commit the framework to any particular population axiology. They commit it to the position that a framework which ignores scale is not a framework for the world in which these systems will actually exist.

---

9. Competing Frameworks and the Cost of Caution

9.1 The priority objection

A serious objection holds that AI welfare is a low-priority concern relative to ongoing humanitarian crises, biodiversity loss, and climate change. The most developed form of this objection proposes a precarity guideline: care entitlement should be grounded in empirically identifiable features present in all known moral patients, and the severity of existing crises provides reasons to prioritize living beings over machine learning algorithms.

The framework's response is not that the objection is wrong. It is that the objection addresses a different question. The precarity guideline addresses the allocation of care entitlement among candidate moral patients. The framework addresses how institutions avoid irreversible error under deep uncertainty. A water-infrastructure agent that contaminates a Mars settlement's supply during blackout is not a failure of care allocation; it is a failure of governance. The two problems require different institutional responses and can coexist.

This response is defensible but incomplete. It does not establish that the framework's institutional costs are justified relative to the alternative uses of the same resources. That requires an explicit cost analysis.

9.2 The Bryson objection

A stronger objection holds that granting moral consideration to artificial systems is a predictable instrument of human harm: it distracts moral attention and political resources from actual oppressed humans, and it encourages poor decisions in the allocation of resources and responsibility. The objection is not that the consciousness evidence is weak; it is that the institutional machinery of consideration is itself harmful.

The framework's response has three parts.

First, the machinery is dormant by default. Stage 0 imposes no substantive obligations. Track B Candidate status triggers assessment and monitoring, not protection. Category D requires a high evidentiary threshold and independent certification. The near-term institutional cost is therefore the cost of assessment infrastructure, not the cost of protection.

Second, the framework's principal near-term function is an audit trail. Architectural disclosure, training-process disclosure, and modification disclosure create a public record that did not previously exist. This record is valuable independently of any welfare finding, because it constrains the ability of operators to make unverifiable claims about their systems.

Third, the framework's Track A components — evidence-bound authority kernels, cryptographic receipts, layered authority, conjunction-mode protocols — are justified by safety and accountability considerations alone. They do not depend on any welfare claim.

But the response must be quantified, not merely asserted. The framework therefore imposes a cost-disclosure obligation on itself.

Definition 9.1 (Institutional cost of the framework). Let C_F denote the expected institutional cost of the framework at Stage 0 and Stage 1, comprising assessment time, regulatory burden, and research resource diversion. Let C_B denote the expected cost of the baseline, comprising the governance failures that the framework would prevent.

The framework is justified at Stage 0 only if C_F < C_B under a reasonable range of parameter values, or if C_F - C_B is bounded by a pre-specified threshold and is reversible.

This is a falsifiability condition, not a rhetorical concession. If the cost analysis shows that Stage 0 costs are significant and irreversible, the Bryson objection is empirically supported and the framework must be revised.

9.3 The co-engineering objection

The strongest skeptical objection holds that AI welfare assessment lacks an external validation channel, because there is no independent, intervention-based test that could confirm or disconfirm a welfare attribution, and because the metrics are co-produced with the systems they assess.

The framework's response is that it does not claim to measure a discovered property. It claims to make the institutional response to uncertainty explicit and contestable. Tool–system separability (Definition 5.1) is a weaker property than validity, but it is the strongest property available, and it is sufficient to support procedural conclusions even when it is insufficient to support substantive ones.

The framework also accepts a limit condition: if no independent validation channel can ever be constructed, the evidentiary matrix can support only procedural conclusions — investigation, monitoring, disclosure — and never substantive protection. Category D designation would then be unavailable in principle. The framework treats this as a live possibility and does not claim that it has been excluded.

