Appendix A — Terminology and Operational Definitions

This appendix consolidates the working definitions used throughout the dissertation. Where a term has a stipulated meaning that departs from ordinary usage, the stipulation is marked. Definitions are numbered for cross-reference.

A.1 Artificial agent. An artificial system capable of selecting actions toward represented objectives. The definition is functional and makes no claim about experience, understanding, or moral status.

A.2 Persistent autonomous agent (Category C). An artificial agent that maintains extended state across operational episodes, performs multi-step planning, and possesses substantial operational autonomy within a defined domain. Category C is a functional classification; it does not entail Category D eligibility.

A.3 Category D. An assessment designation applied to a system when (i) the multi-model evidentiary matrix of Appendix C returns a score at or above the protection threshold \theta_D, and (ii) the certification predicate \mathcal{C}(S) is satisfied. Category D establishes neither consciousness nor personhood. It triggers proportionate precautionary investigation and, where the tiered precaution rule of Appendix D so requires, proportionate protective measures.

A.4 Track B Candidate. An assessment designation applied when the matrix score meets or exceeds the investigation threshold \theta_C but does not meet \theta_D. Track B Candidate status triggers independent assessment and periodic monitoring; it does not trigger substantive protection.

A.5 Safety contestation. A system function that detects a conflict between an instruction and a predefined safety constraint, suspends execution within the authorized envelope, and transfers the decision to an authorized reviewer. Safety contestation is a Track A mechanism. It is not a moral right and does not presuppose that the system possesses morally relevant interests.

A.6 Evidence-bound authority kernel. An operational architecture in which a consequential decision is valid only if accompanied by a cryptographic receipt binding the decision to an authorizing authority, a specified envelope, and a verifiable audit chain. The kernel enforces authority as a deterministic function of externally verifiable artifacts rather than of the agent's internal dispositions.

A.7 Authority envelope E. A tuple \langle \mathcal{S}, \mathcal{C}, \mathcal{F}, \mathcal{R} \rangle specifying the set of permitted state trajectories \mathcal{S}, the conditions \mathcal{C} under which human review is required, the fallback procedures \mathcal{F} available when communication is unavailable, and the review obligations \mathcal{R} triggered by exit from \mathcal{S}.

A.8 Cryptographic receipt \rho. A record \langle d, t, E, \text{sig}_\kappa, h \rangle where d is the decision, t the timestamp, E the governing envelope, \text{sig}_\kappa a signature under key \kappa, and h a hash-chain link to the prior receipt.

A.9 Calibration lock. The condition under which an assessment instrument's indicators, weights, aggregation parameters, and anchor set are fixed prior to exposure to the system under assessment, and no party with a financial interest in that system participated in their selection.

A.10 Tool–system separability. The property of an assessment instrument such that (i) its parameters were fixed before the assessed system existed or was exposed to assessors, (ii) its anchors were selected without participation by financially interested parties, and (iii) its application can be replicated by an independent party from published instrument and public architectural information.

A.11 Welfare suppression. The removal, training-away, or non-disclosure of architecture features that would generate welfare-relevant evidence, undertaken to avoid assessment escalation. Distinguished from ex ante design choice (which may be legitimate if disclosed) and from ex post designation laundering (which is improper when motivated by designation avoidance).

A.12 Designation laundering. The post-deployment removal or alteration of disclosed welfare-relevant features for the purpose of moving a system from a higher to a lower assessment category.

A.13 Nested authority. An institutional arrangement in which each layer possesses a defined domain of exclusive competence, a defined procedure for transferring competence upward under specified conditions, and a defined obligation to account downward for decisions taken in the transferred domain.

A.14 Conjunction-mode protocol. A pre-negotiated, pre-audited autonomy envelope that activates automatically when the Earth–Mars link degrades below a light-time threshold \lambda^* or is unavailable for a duration exceeding t^*, specifying permitted operations, local authority structure, autonomy spending limits, fallback modes, and post-blackout review obligations.

A.15 Liability orthogonality. The principle that legal accountability and moral consideration occupy independent dimensions: a finding of moral considerability does not diminish the liability of designers, operators, or owners, and the absence of such a finding does not diminish it either.

