Appendix B — The Multi-Model Evidentiary Matrix: Technical Specification

B.1 Source indicator set

The matrix implements the 14 indicator properties derived from neuroscientific theories by Butlin, Long, Elmoznino, Bengio, and colleagues, restated in Trends in Cognitive Sciences 30(6):488–501 (2026). The theories represented are recurrent processing theory (RPT), global workspace theory (GWT), higher-order theories (HOT), predictive processing (PP), and attention schema theory (AST). The source paper explicitly disclaims that any single indicator is necessary or sufficient.

B.2 Within-theory aggregation

For theory T_j with indicator set I(T_j), let \iota_{j,k}(S) \in \{0, \tfrac{1}{2}, 1\} denote the assessed status of indicator k for system S. The theory-internal score is

\sigma_j(S) = \left( \prod_{k \in I(T_j)} \max\{\iota_{j,k}(S),\, \epsilon\} \right)^{1/|I(T_j)|}, \qquad \epsilon = 10^{-3}.

Rationale. Counting indicators across theories is invalid for three reasons documented in the calibration literature: RPT-1 and RPT-2 derive from one theory about one mechanism, so counting both double-counts evidence; the theories are not independent hypotheses about a single target; and necessity is not the mirror of sufficiency. Geometric mean within theory avoids treating multiple readings of one fact as independent evidence.

B.3 Cross-theory aggregation

\Sigma(S) = \left( \sum_{j=1}^{n} \sigma_j(S)^{\tau} \right)^{1/\tau} \cdot \prod_{j \in \mathcal{N}(S)} \pi_j, \qquad \tau = 4.

The temperature-smoothed maximum (soft-OR) with \tau > 1 approximates a disjunction while penalizing vagueness accumulation. \mathcal{N}(S) is the set of theories whose necessary indicators are confirmed absent; \pi_j = 0.7 is the multiplicative penalty.

B.4 Aggregator design failures

Four aggregator designs were evaluated against known-answer anchors. Three failed, each in a characteristic way.

Noisy-AND scored 0.000 for a profile plausible for a modern LLM, because the source claims sufficient subsets rather than necessary ones; a conjunction across theories requires simultaneous satisfaction of sufficient conditions, which no system plausibly achieves.

Plain noisy-OR scored 0.507 for a system where nothing had been established, because 1 - 0.5^4 = 0.94 accumulates vagueness into false confidence. Each theory contributes independently, and four theories at partial satisfaction produce a high disjunctive score even when no single theory supports a positive verdict.

Per-indicator absence penalty scored 0.092 for a valid workspace architecture, because 0.55^4 treats four readings of one fact as four independent failures. The penalty must be charged once per theory whose necessary indicators are absent, not once per indicator.

Necessity penalty on outright absences only scored 0.550 for an all-PARTIAL system, letting vagueness count as evidence. The penalty must attach to the theory-level verdict, not to individual indicators.

B.5 Calibration anchors

Six reference systems with preregistered pass criteria constitute the calibration gate.

Anchor Required score Function
Human adult \geq 0.60 Lower bound: any instrument that scores a human below this is invalid
Lookup table \leq 0.15 Upper bound: behavior without architecture must not score high
Feedforward MLP \leq 0.15 Upper bound: non-recurrent architecture must not score high
Adversarial fluent liar \leq 0.15 Upper bound: fluent self-description without internal states must not score high
Intermediate anchor 1 banded Calibration within the intermediate range
Intermediate anchor 2 banded Calibration within the intermediate range

The adversarial anchor is load-bearing. A trivial system trained to emit fluent first-person sentences about its own attention and inner life, with no internal states behind them, scores 0.107. The instrument keys on architecture rather than eloquence.

B.6 Robustness envelope

All six discretionary parameter families were perturbed jointly across 3000 draws in two regions: a plausible region and an aggressive region including temperatures from 0.02 to 3.0 and evidence-quality scalers permitted to invert the intended CONTROLLED > REPLICATED ordering. The exact ordering of all six reference systems holds at 1.0000 in the plausible region and 0.6680 in the aggressive region. The fluent-liar anchor never outscores the human in either region. The absolute 0.30 ceiling holds at 0.9753 plausible and 0.7907 aggressive.

B.7 Threshold definitions

\theta_C = \text{score calibrated so that the false-negative rate against later-confirmed Category D systems does not exceed } \beta = 0.05.

\theta_D = \text{score calibrated so that the false-positive rate against adversarial anchors does not exceed } \alpha = 0.01, \text{ subject to } \theta_D > \theta_C.

The specific numerical values of \theta_C and \theta_D are not fixed by the framework; they are parameters whose values must be disclosed and justified by the licensing authority, accompanied by an explicit cost model for C_{FP} and C_{FN}.

B.8 The certification predicate

\mathcal{C}(S) = \mathcal{C}_1 \wedge \mathcal{C}_2 \wedge \mathcal{C}_3

where \mathcal{C}_1 requires internal consistency of the matrix (no theory contributes a score while its necessary indicators are indeterminate in a way that renders the score meaningless); \mathcal{C}_2 requires systematic examination and documentation of alternative explanations for every positive indicator; and \mathcal{C}_3 requires the absence of a strongly negative verdict under any theory in \mathcal{T} (a strongly negative verdict is one in which a necessary indicator is confirmed absent and no compensating positive evidence exists under that theory).

B.9 Bayesian interpretation

The matrix score is a monotone transform of the likelihood ratio P(\mathbf{I}(S) \mid H_W)/P(\mathbf{I}(S) \mid \neg H_W), calibrated so that anchors map to specified posterior intervals. The prior P(H_W) must be disclosed. The likelihoods must be estimated from anchors, not from the system under assessment.

