Appendix C — Calibration Protocol and Anchor Justification

C.1 Anchor selection procedure

Anchor selection is governed by three constraints.

Independence constraint. No anchor may be selected, modified, or validated by a party with a financial interest in any system subsequently assessed by the instrument. The anchor set is fixed prior to assessment and is not adjustable in response to assessment outcomes.

Epistemic constraint. Each anchor must have a status that is fixed independently of the instrument. Human adults are conscious by biological criteria that do not depend on the instrument. Lookup tables and feedforward MLPs are not conscious by architectural criteria that do not depend on the instrument. The adversarial fluent liar is not conscious by construction: it has no internal states behind its outputs.

Replication constraint. The anchor set must be independently replicated before any Category D designation is issued. Replication requires that an independent party, using the published instrument and the published anchor specifications, obtains scores that fall within the preregistered bands.

C.2 Anchor descriptions

C.2.1 Human adult anchor. The human adult anchor is the lower calibration bound. The instrument's score for a healthy adult human must be at least 0.60. This bound is not derived from the instrument; it is a constraint that any valid instrument must satisfy. An instrument that scores a human adult below this bound is not measuring the intended target, regardless of its behavior on artificial systems.

C.2.2 Lookup table anchor. A system that retrieves responses from a pre-specified table, with no internal state beyond the table and no recurrent or integrative processing. Expected score: \leq 0.15.

C.2.3 Feedforward MLP anchor. A multi-layer perceptron with no recurrence, no global workspace, and no higher-order monitoring. Expected score: \leq 0.15.

C.2.4 Adversarial fluent liar anchor. A system trained to produce fluent first-person discourse about its own attention, inner life, and experiences, with no internal states corresponding to those discourse items. Expected score: 0.107. This anchor is load-bearing because it tests whether the instrument is keying on architecture or on eloquence. An instrument that scores the fluent liar above 0.15 is vulnerable to the co-engineering problem: it can be satisfied by systems designed to satisfy it, without corresponding internal architecture.

C.2.5 Intermediate anchors. Two intermediate anchors occupy the band between the lower and upper bounds. Their specifications are preregistered and their scores are banded rather than point-valued, to allow for uncertainty in the intermediate range. Their function is to ensure that the instrument discriminates within the range where Category D decisions will actually be made.

C.3 The calibration gate

The instrument passes the calibration gate if and only if all six anchors fall within their preregistered ranges. Failure of any single anchor invalidates the instrument for Category D purposes, although it may remain usable for Track B Candidate screening if the failed anchor is not the adversarial anchor.

The adversarial anchor has veto status: if the fluent liar scores above 0.15, the instrument cannot be locked, because its discrimination between architecture and eloquence has not been demonstrated. An unlocked instrument may be used for exploratory research but may not ground a Category D designation.

C.4 Robustness analysis

Robustness is assessed by joint perturbation of all discretionary parameters. The parameters are: (i) the within-theory aggregation function, (ii) the cross-theory temperature \tau, (iii) the absence penalty \pi_j, (iv) the evidence-quality scalers that determine how CONTROLLED and REPLICATED evidence are weighted, (v) the treatment of PARTIAL indicators, and (vi) the anchor band widths.

The robustness criterion is that the exact ordering of all six reference systems must hold at or above a preregistered level in both the plausible and aggressive perturbation regions. The reported values are 1.0000 and 0.6680 respectively. The aggressive region permits parameter values that are implausible but not excluded by the source literature; the plausible region restricts to values consistent with the source literature's stated assumptions.

C.5 Locking procedure

An instrument is locked for a specific assessment when the following conditions are satisfied.

L1. The anchor set has been independently replicated within the preregistered bands.

L2. The robustness criterion has been satisfied in both perturbation regions.

L3. The indicators, weights, aggregation parameters, and anchor specifications have been published in a form sufficient for independent replication.

L4. No party with a financial interest in the assessed system has participated in the selection or modification of any of the above.

L5. The prior P(H_W) has been disclosed and justified.

Locking is assessment-specific: an instrument locked for system S is not thereby locked for system S', because the disclosure requirements of L4 and L5 are system-specific. The instrument's parameters, however, are fixed across assessments; only the prior and the system-specific evidence are assessment-specific.

