Appendix D — Formal Derivations

D.1 Contamination bias in self-report likelihood ratios

Let R denote the event that a system self-reports a welfare-relevant state and W the event that the corresponding state is present. Let T denote the training objective. The observed likelihood ratio is

\Lambda_{\text{obs}} = \frac{P(R \mid W)}{P(R \mid \neg W)}.

Decompose by the training objective:

P(R \mid W) = P(R \mid W, T_{\text{pen}}) P(T_{\text{pen}} \mid W) + P(R \mid W, \neg T_{\text{pen}}) P(\neg T_{\text{pen}} \mid W).

If T_{\text{pen}} is a training objective that penalizes R when W is present, then

P(R \mid W, T_{\text{pen}}) < P(R \mid W, \neg T_{\text{pen}}).

Similarly for P(R \mid \neg W). The observed ratio is therefore biased toward the ratio under the penalizing objective, which is closer to unity than the ratio under no penalization:

\Lambda_{\text{obs}} \to \frac{P(R \mid W, T_{\text{pen}})}{P(R \mid \neg W, T_{\text{pen}})} \approx 1.

Under a training objective that fully suppresses R when W is present, \Lambda_{\text{obs}} approaches 1 regardless of the diagnostic value of self-report under non-penalizing conditions. This establishes Proposition 5.1: the absence of self-report is not evidence of absence, and may be weak evidence of presence, because the training objective that suppresses R may be more likely to be applied to systems that would otherwise exhibit R.

D.2 Minimax regret over moral theories

Let \mathcal{M} = \{M_1, \dots, M_m\} be a set of moral theories, each assigning a value V_k(a) to action a. The regret of a under M_k is

R_k(a) = \max_{a'} V_k(a') - V_k(a).

The minimax-regret action is

a^* = \arg\min_{a} \max_{k} R_k(a).

Proposition D.1. Minimax regret satisfies the independence of irrelevant alternatives (IIA) if the value functions are invariant to the addition of irrelevant alternatives.

Proof. Suppose a is preferred to b under minimax regret for a feasible set A, and let c \notin A. Then c is irrelevant to the ranking of a and b if c does not change \max_{a'} V_k(a') for any k. If c does change the maximum, then the regret of both a and b increases by the same amount under every theory, so the ranking is preserved. Hence minimax regret satisfies IIA under value-invariance. \square

The framework does not require IIA to hold. The proposition is stated to clarify that minimax regret is not vulnerable to the standard IIA objection under the value-invariance condition, which is the condition under which the framework applies it.

D.3 The tiered precaution rule as a constrained optimization

Let r(a) \in [0,1] denote reversibility, s(a) \in [0,1] severity of welfare harm, and H(a) \in \{0,1\} the human-safety constraint. The feasible set is

\mathcal{F} = \{a : H(a) = 1\}.

The tiered rule partitions \mathcal{F} into three regions:

\mathcal{P}_1 = \{a \in \mathcal{F} : r(a) \geq r^* \wedge s(a) \leq s^*\},

\mathcal{P}_2 = \{a \in \mathcal{F} : r(a) < r^* \vee s(a) > s^*\},

\mathcal{P}_3 = \{a : H(a) = 0\}.

Actions in \mathcal{P}_1 are permitted by default. Actions in \mathcal{P}_2 require Category D status and certification. Actions in \mathcal{P}_3 are prohibited regardless of evidentiary status.

Proposition D.2. The tiered rule is a lexicographic ordering: H(a) is a hard constraint, r(a) and s(a) are secondary criteria, and evidentiary status is tertiary.

Proof. By construction, H(a) = 0 excludes a from \mathcal{F} regardless of r, s, or evidentiary status. Within \mathcal{F}, the partition into \mathcal{P}_1 and \mathcal{P}_2 is determined by r and s, and evidentiary status is relevant only for \mathcal{P}_2. Hence the ordering is lexicographic. \square

The thresholds r^* and s^* encode substantive normative commitments. The framework does not derive them; it requires that they be disclosed and subjected to sensitivity analysis.

D.4 Conjunction-mode authority transfer

Let \lambda(t) be instantaneous one-way light-time and u(t) the link availability indicator. Authority transfers to the local commander when

\lambda(t) > \lambda^* \;\;\vee\;\; \int_{t-t^*}^{t} (1 - u(s))\, ds > t^*.

The integral condition captures cumulative unavailability: a link that is nominally available but degraded for a sufficient fraction of the window triggers transfer. The thresholds \lambda^* and t^* are set by the licensing authority, not the operator, because they determine when the operator's authority is suspended.

Proposition D.3. If the operator sets \lambda^* or t^*, the operator can prevent authority transfer by setting both thresholds arbitrarily high. Authority transfer is therefore only credible if the thresholds are set externally.

This is a straightforward incentive argument: an operator that bears the cost of local autonomy has an incentive to avoid triggering it. External setting of the thresholds removes that incentive.

D.5 Institutional cost bound

Let C_F denote the expected institutional cost of the framework at Stage 0 and Stage 1, and C_B the expected cost of the baseline governance failures the framework would prevent. The framework is justified at Stage 0 if

C_F < C_B \;\;\vee\;\; (C_F - C_B) \leq \Delta \wedge \text{reversible},

where \Delta is a pre-specified threshold. The second disjunct allows for a bounded, reversible excess cost in exchange for option value: the framework preserves the capacity to escalate if evidence emerges. The option value is not quantified in the framework; it is a qualitative justification for tolerating a bounded reversible excess cost.

